1. Title and Authors of the Paper
Title: Landsat-based greening trends in alpine ecosystems are inflated by multidecadal increases in summer observations
Authors: Arthur Bayle, Simon Gascoin, Logan T. Berner, and Philippe Choler
Published: Ecography 2024: e07394 (accepted 22 July 2024)

2. Quick Overview


Why is it relevant? Landsat-derived vegetation greening trends are widely used to document ecological responses to climate change, yet the satellite's inconsistent observation frequency over time could introduce systematic biases that inflate these trends --- a problem not previously quantified in terms of its impact on trend magnitude or its variation across habitats.


What was done? The authors combined MODIS-derived phenological curves with actual Landsat observation availability over 38 years (1984--2021) to simulate and quantify "false greening trends" arising purely from increasing observation density, then modeled how these biases vary with growing season length and observation count across the European Alps.


What is the main outcome? Almost 50\% of estimated Landsat greening at high elevations (above 2400 m) in the European Alps can be attributed to sampling bias, with late-snowmelting habitats like alpine snowbeds being most affected, calling for corrections or caution when interpreting and comparing greening trends across habitats.



3. Main Goal and Fundamental Concept
The primary objective was to quantify how the increasing number of available Landsat observations over recent decades creates spurious positive trends in annual maximum NDVI (NDVImax) --- a widely used proxy for vegetation productivity --- and to determine which environmental and phenological factors govern the magnitude of this bias. The fundamental concept is straightforward: when using maximum-value compositing to estimate annual peak greenness, having more observations in recent years increases the probability of capturing the true seasonal peak, systematically inflating NDVImax estimates over time and producing apparent greening trends even where no actual vegetation change has occurred.

4. Technical Approach
The analysis proceeded in three parts:
Part 1 --- Quantifying false greening trends: MODIS 250-m, 8-day composites (2000--2021) were used to characterize vegetation phenology across the European Alps ({\textgreater}1500 m elevation, non-forested, tree cover {\textless}5\%). Over one million MODIS pixels were processed: raw NDVI was filtered (BISE algorithm + Savitzky-Golay smoothing), interpolated to daily values, and clustered into three phenological types (early, intermediate, and late snowmelting sites) using partitioning around medoids (PAM). Seven-parameter double logistic functions were fitted to each cluster's phenology. Separately, the actual annual count of clear-sky, snow-free Landsat observations during the growing season (DOY 152--243) was extracted from Landsat Collection 2 (TM, ETM+, OLI) using Google Earth Engine. To generate false trends, a 38-year stable (trendless) NDVI time series was created by repeating the same phenological curve annually, then subsampled using the actual Landsat observation counts per year. The resulting NDVImax time series were analyzed for trends using the Theil-Sen estimator --- any detected trend is purely a sampling artifact.
Part 2 --- Modeling drivers of bias magnitude: 100 phenological clusters $\times$ 100,000 observation time series = 10 million false trend estimates were generated. A random forest regression model predicted false trend magnitude from two variables: growing season length (GSL) and cumulative observation count ($\Sigma$OBS\_GS).
Part 3 --- Mapping bias across habitats: The random forest model was applied pixel-wise across the European Alps to map false trends along elevation and floristic gradients. The relative importance of false trends was computed by comparing them to actual observed Landsat greening trends (1984--2021), after cross-calibrating Landsat 7/8 reflectances and correcting for BRDF effects. Floristic validation used 11,114 vegetation plots from the French National Alpine Botanical Conservatory, clustered into 12 habitat types.

5. Distinctive Features


First quantification of bias impact on trend magnitude: Previous work (Berner et al. 2020) established the asymptotic relationship between observation count and NDVImax estimation, but this study is the first to quantify how this bias translates into false greening trends and to model the factors controlling their magnitude.


Habitat-specific bias assessment: By linking the bias to floristic clusters derived from independent botanical survey data, the study reveals which specific plant communities (e.g., snowbed communities, alpine screes) are most affected --- directly relevant to conservation prioritization.


Demonstration that alternative metrics don't solve the problem: The authors tested quantile-based metrics (median, q75, q80, q90) instead of maximum NDVI and found all were similarly affected by the sampling bias, undermining a commonly assumed workaround.


Transferability to other cold ecosystems: Supporting analyses for the Arctic tundra and Asian mountain ranges demonstrate that the same mechanisms operate globally wherever short growing seasons coincide with increasing Landsat observation density.



6. Experimental Setup and Results
Data: MODIS MOD09QA 250-m composites (2000--2021) for phenology; Landsat Collection 2 (5 TM, 7 ETM+, 8 OLI) clear-sky snow-free observations (1984--2021); EU-DEM 25-m elevation; Copernicus tree cover density; 11,114 floristic plots from the French Alps (CBNA database).
Key results:


NDVImax underestimation with one observation averaged 30\% for late-snowmelting sites versus 11\% for early-snowmelting sites, confirming the asymptotic relationship.


False greening trends (purely from sampling bias) averaged 0.00054 NDVI/year for late-snowmelting sites vs. 0.00025 for early-snowmelting sites --- a relative false trend of 7.5\% vs. 2\% of true NDVImax over 38 years.


A random forest model explained 96\% of variance in false trend magnitude using just two predictors: cumulative observation count ($\Sigma$OBS\_GS, slightly more important) and growing season length (GSL). False trends were highest with short growing seasons and few observations.


Along the elevation gradient: false trends increased sharply above 2300 m, reaching {\textasciitilde}0.001 NDVI/year at 2600 m. The relative importance of false trends was 10--20\% between 1500--2400 m, then accelerated to 20--50\% between 2400--2800 m.


Among floristic clusters: north-exposed alpine screes had the highest relative bias importance (median 30\%), while forest ecotone communities had the lowest (8\%).


These results may explain why Rumpf et al. (2022), using Landsat, found 77\% of alpine surfaces greening compared to 55\% in MODIS-based studies.



7. Advantages and Limitations
Advantages:


Provides the first systematic, quantitative framework for assessing how Landsat observation heterogeneity inflates vegetation greening trends, filling an important methodological gap.


The random forest model (R$^2$ = 0.96) offers a practical tool for estimating bias magnitude at any location given GSL and observation count, enabling post-hoc correction of published results.


The simulation approach --- using real observation patterns applied to trendless phenologies --- cleanly isolates the sampling bias from actual ecological change.


Results are broadly generalizable to all cold, seasonally snow-covered ecosystems worldwide.


Limitations:


The analysis assumed static phenology (same curve repeated annually) and did not account for actual long-term changes in growing season length or snow cover duration, which could either amplify or partially offset the bias.


MODIS (250 m) was used for phenology while Landsat operates at 30 m --- the scale mismatch may smooth out fine-scale phenological heterogeneity in complex terrain.


The study focused on linear trends over the full 38-year Landsat record, while observation increases occurred non-linearly (step changes at Landsat 7 and 8 launches), potentially creating false non-linear greening patterns not captured by the linear trend analysis.


The analysis was focused on non-forested alpine vegetation above 1500 m; applicability to forested ecosystems or lower elevations was not assessed, though the mechanisms are general.


The study does not invalidate overall alpine greening --- false trends were always smaller than observed trends --- but quantifying the residual "true" greening after correction remains an open challenge.



8. Conclusion
Bayle et al. (2024) demonstrate a critical but underappreciated methodological issue: Landsat-based greening trends in seasonally snow-covered ecosystems are systematically inflated by the increasing number of satellite observations over time. The magnitude of this "false greening" varies predictably with growing season length and observation density, reaching up to 50\% of observed trends in high-elevation alpine habitats with short growing seasons and few observations --- precisely the habitats most threatened by climate change and most in need of reliable monitoring. The study does not challenge the reality of alpine greening, which is supported by multiple independent lines of evidence, but reveals that the magnitude and spatial pattern of Landsat-derived trends require correction, particularly when comparing across habitats or elevation gradients. The authors urge the remote sensing and ecology communities to report observation metadata, apply bias corrections (such as the phenology-based approach in the LandsatTS R package), and exercise caution when interpreting greening trends from Landsat time series in any environment with seasonal snow cover and variable observation density.
},
	file = {Ecography - 2024 - Bayle - Landsat-based greening trends in alpine ecosystems are inflated by multidecadal increases in:/home/bene/Desktop/repos/literature/Ecography - 2024 - Bayle - Landsat-based greening trends in alpine ecosystems are inflated by multidecadal increases in.pdf:application/pdf},
}

