1. Title and Authors of the Paper
Title: Aridity-induced phenological shifts and greening trends in Mediterranean forest species: Insights from 28 years of Landsat data in southern Spain
Authors: Aurelio D. Herraiz, Pablo Salazar-Zarzosa, Cristina Acosta-Mu{\~n}oz, Roc{\'i}o Hern{\'a}ndez-Clemente, and Rafael Villar
Published: Ecological Indicators 171, 113115 (2025)

2. Quick Overview


Why is it relevant? Mediterranean forests face intensifying aridity under climate change, yet species-specific phenological responses to aridity gradients remain poorly characterized, particularly in southern Europe where water availability --- rather than temperature --- primarily drives vegetation activity.


What was done? The authors analyzed 28 years (1994--2021) of monthly maximum NDVI from Landsat for 2,358 Spanish National Forest Inventory plots across 10 Mediterranean forest species in Andalusia, extracting phenological metrics and relating them to an aridity index.


What is the main outcome? Most species showed a general greening trend over time, but aridity differentially impacts phenology across species --- delaying the start and end of the growing season, reducing NDVI values, and shortening the growth season of some species (Scots pine) while causing phenological shifts in others (Cork oak, Stone pine, Aleppo pine), revealing species-specific vulnerabilities to increasing aridity.



3. Main Goal and Fundamental Concept
The primary objective was to characterize how aridity affects the phenological cycles of the ten most common Mediterranean forest species in southern Spain and to track temporal trends in their vegetation greenness over nearly three decades. The core concept is that in Mediterranean ecosystems --- unlike northern temperate forests where temperature is the primary phenological driver --- water availability (captured by an aridity index integrating both temperature and precipitation) governs the timing and intensity of the growing season, and different species exhibit distinct adaptive responses to aridity gradients.

4. Technical Approach
The study used 2,358 permanent plots from the third Spanish National Forest Inventory in Andalusia, filtered for plots with {\textgreater}90\% dominant species biomass and {\textgreater}150 trees/ha. Monthly maximum NDVI was extracted from Landsat 5 TM (1994--2000) and Landsat 7 ETM+ (2001--2021) at 30-m resolution via Google Earth Engine, totaling over 5,000 satellite scenes across 28 years. Linear interpolation filled data gaps from clouds and satellite failures.
The hydrometeorological year (September 1 -- August 30) was used to better reflect Mediterranean vegetation activity. Time series processing used the Rbeast package for decomposition into seasonal, trend, and residual components, and the Greenbrown R package (derivative approach with spline smoothing) for extracting phenological metrics: SOS (Start of Season), EOS (End of Season), LOS (Length of Season), POP (Position of Peak), POT (Position of Trough), PEAK, TROUGH, MSP (NDVI at SOS), and MAU (NDVI at EOS). Circular statistics were applied for cyclical metrics (SOS, EOS, POP, POT).
Aridity was quantified using a modified Martonne index transformed to an aridity index (AI = 150 $-$ MI), based on 20-year climate averages from WorldClim 2.1. The effect of aridity on phenological metrics was assessed through linear regressions (phenometric values averaged per plot over time vs. aridity). Temporal trends in NDVI and phenological metrics were analyzed using the trend component from time series decomposition and correlations with time over the 28-year period. Analyses were conducted separately for each of the 10 species.

5. Distinctive Features


Species-level phenological analysis across an aridity gradient: Rather than treating Mediterranean forests as a homogeneous unit, the study resolves phenological responses for 10 individual species (1 deciduous, 9 evergreen) spanning a wide aridity range (AI 28--119), revealing species-specific vulnerabilities.


Mediterranean-adapted phenological framework: By using the hydrometeorological year and circular statistics for cyclical metrics, the study appropriately captures the distinctive Mediterranean phenological pattern where the growing season starts in autumn (with rains) and ends in late spring (with drought onset) --- the inverse of temperate Northern Hemisphere patterns.


Aridity as an integrated climate predictor: Using an aridity index that combines temperature and precipitation avoids the limitation of analyzing these climate variables in isolation, providing a more ecologically meaningful predictor for water-limited systems.


Long time series at fine spatial resolution: The 28-year Landsat record at 30 m resolution, anchored to forest inventory plot locations, provides temporal depth unavailable from newer sensors (Sentinel) while maintaining spatial resolution appropriate for individual forest stands.



6. Experimental Setup and Results
Data: 2,358 Spanish National Forest Inventory plots in Andalusia; Landsat 5 TM and 7 ETM+ NDVI (1994--2021); 10 species: P. halepensis (24.8\%), Q. ilex (18.9\%), P. pinea (13.6\%), P. nigra (12\%), P. pinaster (11.5\%), Q. suber (10.2\%), P. sylvestris (4.1\%), O. europaea (2.8\%), E. camaldulensis (1.3\%), C. sativa (0.9\%).
Key results:
Phenological characterization: Evergreen species showed SOS in autumn (October), NDVI peaks in winter (December--February), and EOS in late spring (March--May), with NDVI minima in summer --- confirming that precipitation, not temperature, drives the Mediterranean growing season. The deciduous C. sativa showed the opposite pattern (winter minima, summer maxima). Mountain pines (P. nigra, P. sylvestris) had shorter growing seasons ({\textasciitilde}160 days) versus {\textasciitilde}180 days for lowland species and {\textasciitilde}210 days for C. sativa.
Aridity effects: All Pinus and Quercus species showed delayed SOS with increasing aridity. Several species (P. halepensis, P. nigra, P. pinaster, P. sylvestris, Q. suber) also delayed EOS in more arid areas. LOS responses were species-specific: P. sylvestris, P. pinea, E. camaldulensis, and O. europaea shortened their growing season under aridity, while P. halepensis and P. pinaster actually lengthened it. For most species, higher aridity was associated with lower NDVI values (PEAK, TROUGH, MSP, MAU), except for mountain pines and C. sativa.
Temporal trends (1994--2021): All species except E. camaldulensis showed significant increases in NDVI over time (greening). Two patterns emerged: (1) continuous increase throughout the period (C. sativa, Q. ilex, P. halepensis, P. nigra), and (2) increase until {\textasciitilde}2005 followed by stabilization (E. camaldulensis, O. europaea, Q. suber, P. pinaster, P. pinea). NDVI-related metrics (PEAK, TROUGH, MSP, MAU) showed positive temporal trends for most species, but temporal phenological metrics (SOS, EOS, LOS) remained stable over time, suggesting phenological cycle timing is resilient despite increasing greenness.

7. Advantages and Limitations
Advantages:


Species-specific analysis across a strong climatic gradient within a single region provides ecologically meaningful insights that pooled analyses would obscure.


The 28-year time series provides sufficient temporal depth for detecting both trends and stabilization phases in vegetation dynamics.


Using forest inventory plots with known species composition and structure ensures NDVI signals are attributable to specific dominant species.


The integrated aridity index captures the combined temperature-precipitation effects most relevant to water-limited Mediterranean systems.


Limitations:


Landsat's 16-day revisit time and cloud contamination limit phenological precision; monthly maximum compositing may smooth out fine-scale phenological events.


The study did not account for the Landsat observation density bias described by Bayle et al. (2024), where increasing observation availability over time can inflate NDVI trends --- a potentially relevant concern for the greening trends reported.


The 30-m pixel does not perfectly match the 50-m diameter inventory plots, though the authors demonstrated high correlation (R$^2$ = 0.93) between single-pixel and multi-pixel NDVI.


Soil, topography, management history, and understory vegetation were not fully controlled for, though sensitivity analyses suggested minor understory effects on temporal trends.


The aridity index was based on 20-year climate averages rather than year-specific values, limiting the ability to capture interannual aridity variability effects on phenology.


Mixing Landsat 5 TM and 7 ETM+ data could introduce subtle spectral inconsistencies, though the authors cited high inter-sensor correlation.



8. Conclusion
Herraiz et al. (2025) provide a detailed, species-resolved analysis of how aridity shapes the phenological cycles and greenness trajectories of Mediterranean forest species in southern Spain over 28 years. The key finding is that while a general greening trend persists across most species, aridity impacts phenology in highly species-specific ways --- delaying season onset and reducing vegetation greenness for most species, shortening the growing season of some (notably P. sylvestris), and causing phenological shifts in others. The study highlights that drought-adapted species like P. halepensis and Q. ilex show continued positive NDVI trajectories and may be resilient to projected aridity increases, while cold-habitat (P. sylvestris) and humidity-dependent (Q. suber) species show signs of vulnerability. These species-specific responses have direct implications for reforestation policies and adaptive forest management in the Mediterranean under climate change. The work demonstrates the value of combining long-term Landsat archives with forest inventory data for monitoring species-level responses to aridity, though future analyses should account for potential satellite observation biases and incorporate finer-resolution climate data.
},
	file = {1-s2.0-S1470160X25000445-main:/home/bene/Desktop/repos/literature/1-s2.0-S1470160X25000445-main.pdf:application/pdf},
}

